Section 4: Few-Shot vs Zero-Shot Judgment 
_____________________________________________
Q7. Classification with Edge Cases

Zero-shot prompt
You are a customer support analyst at a telecom company. Your job is to classify the customer complaint below into one category: Billing, Network, Device or Other. Please find the complaint below,
[Customer complaint]
If the complaint mentions more than one issue, pick the one the customer most wants resolved and mention the second issue in one short line. Give the output as Category, Reason and Confidence (low/medium/high), in a neutral tone and under 50 words.
Use only the complaint. Don’t invent details. Use only the four categories given. If the complaint doesn’t clearly fit Billing, Network or Device, choose Other. If it is too unclear to judge, say so in the reason and set the confidence to low.

Few-shot prompt
You are a customer support analyst at a telecom company. Your job is to classify the customer complaint below into one category: Billing, Network or Device, or Other. Here are some examples of how to classify,
Complaint: “I was charged twice this month.” Category: Billing.
Complaint: “No signal at my house since Monday.” Category: Network.
Complaint: “My phone screen is cracked and won’t turn on.” Category: Device.
Complaint: “My bill is wrong and the internet keeps dropping.” Category: Billing, because the bill is the first and most concrete issue. Second issue: Network.
Complaint: “My new phone can’t connect to your network.” Category: Device, because the problem is the phone, not the network. Second issue: Network.
Complaint: “I want to know about your offers.” Category: Other.
Please find the complaint below,
[Customer complaint]
Give the output as Category, Reason and Confidence (low/medium/high), under 50 words. Follow the pattern of the examples, use only the complaint, and don’t invent details. If it is too unclear to judge, say so and set the confidence to low.

1. Why this structure?
Role: a support analyst, so the model focuses on sorting complaints.
Context: the four categories and the rule for overlapping issues tell the model how to handle messy cases.
Instruction: pick one category and mention a second issue if there is one.
Output format: Category, Reason and Confidence keep the answer short and easy to compare.
Tone: neutral.
Guardrails: no invented details, only the four categories, and low confidence when unclear.

2. Why this technique?
Both are shown because the task is simple but the boundaries are blurry. Few-shot helps here because the examples show where the lines fall, such as a new phone that can’t connect being Device and not Network. A written rule alone can leave the model guessing on those cases.
It breaks when the examples don’t cover the new complaint, when one example is wrongly labelled (the error spreads to every answer), or when the model copies the wording of the examples too closely. It can also lean towards the category that appears most often in the examples. Zero-shot is cheaper and shorter, but it gives less consistent answers on overlapping cases.

3. Failure Modes Prevented
- Inconsistent labels on overlapping cases
- Inventing new categories
- Ignoring the second issue
- Guessing on unclear complaints
- Copying the examples too closely

4. Alternative prompt design
Zero-shot prompt
You are a customer support analyst at a telecom company who routes complaints to the right team. Your job is to classify the complaint below into Billing, Network, Device or Other. Please find the complaint below,
[Customer complaint]
Give a primary category and, if another issue is clearly mentioned, a secondary category, each with a one-line reason. Add a confidence level (low/medium/high) and keep the output under 60 words, in a neutral tone.
Use only the complaint and don’t invent details. Use only the four categories given. If the complaint is about a general question or doesn’t fit the first three, choose Other. If it is too unclear to judge, write “Unclear” and set the confidence to low.

Few-shot prompt
You are a support analyst. Your job is to classify the customer complaint below as Billing, Network, Device or Other. Here are two tricky examples of how to decide,
Complaint: “My bill is wrong and the internet keeps dropping.” Category: Billing, because the bill is the first and most concrete issue.
Complaint: “My new phone can’t connect to your network.” Category: Device, because the problem is the phone and not the network.
Please find the complaint below,
[Customer complaint]
Give the category and a one-line reason. Use only the complaint and say “Unclear” if you can’t judge.
